```c
void shortest_to_front(int n, char *s[static n]) {
    int min = 0;                     // index of the shortest string so far
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if (strlen(s[i]) < strlen(s[min])) {
            min = i;
        }
    }
    char *tmp = s[0];                // swap the pointers
    s[0] = s[min];
    s[min] = tmp;
}
```

Tested on the example (gives `bat, anteater, zebra, frog`) and on arrays whose shortest string is already first.

```c
void largest_to_back(int n, char *w[static n]) {
    int max = 0;                     // index of the alphabetically last string so far
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if (strcmp(w[i], w[max]) > 0) {
            max = i;
        }
    }
    char *tmp = w[n - 1];            // swap the pointers
    w[n - 1] = w[max];
    w[max] = tmp;
}
```

Tested on the example (gives `pear, kiwi, apple, zucchini`) and on arrays whose largest string is already last.

```c
void sort_by_length(int n, char *s[static n]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        shortest_to_front(n - i, s + i);   // or &s[i]
    }
}
```

Each call puts the shortest of the remaining strings `s[i] … s[n-1]` at position i, by passing the sub-array that starts at `s + i` (a pointer to `s[i]`) with length `n - i`. Tested on the example: gives `bat, frog, zebra, anteater`.

```c
void sort_words(int n, char *w[static n]) {
    for (int k = n; k > 1; k--) {
        largest_to_back(k, w);
    }
}
```

Each call moves the largest of the first k strings to position k-1, then the next call only looks at the first k-1. Tested on `["pear", "zucchini", "apple", "kiwi"]`: gives `apple, kiwi, pear, zucchini`.

