questions.prompt:
Write the `main` function of a C program that requires one command-line argument which is a file name. If one command-line argument is not given, then the program should print a usage message to standard error and exit with an exit status of 2.

The input file is a plain-text file containing words separated by whitespace; each word is fewer than 64 characters long. The program should read the words into an array of strings (using `read_words`), sort the stored words alphabetically (using `sort_words`), print the stored words in sorted order, one per line, and finally print a line `N words in file` where N is the total number of words in the file (which may be more than the number stored).

The functions you must use are:

```c
// opens filename, stores the first 50 words of the file in w[0], w[1], ...
// (each w[i] must already have room for 64 chars), closes the file,
// and returns the actual number of words in the file
int read_words(const char *filename, char *w[static 50]);

// sorts the n strings in w alphabetically
void sort_words(int n, char *w[static n]);
```

questions.answer:
```c
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "usage: %s filename\n", argv[0]);
        exit(2);
    }
    char words[50][64];   // each word is fewer than 64 characters
    char *w[50];
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        w[i] = words[i];
    }
    int total = read_words(argv[1], w);
    int n = total < 50 ? total : 50;
    if (n > 0) {
        sort_words(n, w);
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%s\n", w[i]);
    }
    printf("%d words in file\n", total);
    return 0;
}
```

Tested with 9 words, an empty file (prints `0 words in file`), 60 words (prints the first 50 sorted, then `60 words in file`), and no argument (usage message, exit status 2).
